  "query_preview": "..\n\nSYNOPTIC SETUP:\n\nA ridge is building over the Northern Rockies and will gradually \nshift east into the CWA, bringing warmer and drier conditions \nacross Northeast Montana. Temperatures are expected to rise into \nthe mid- to-upper 60s into the 70s through Friday before a \ndeepening trough ejects eastward into the region. Winds are \nexpected to increase out of the east tonight going into Wednesday \nmorning and afternoon. \n\nThe next disturbance is developing over the Pacific Northwest and\nwill continue to drop south into northern California Wednesday and\nThursday before ejecting northeast into the Northern Rockies late\nThursday into Friday. Increasing moisture and lift should support\nchances for widespread showers and possible thunderstorms going\ninto the weekend. Heading into Monday, models are showing the low\npressure to cut-off and drift eastward out of the CWA.\n\nRainfall totals could approach between 0.50\" to 1.00\" in eastern \nportions of the CWA, while places in the western zones could see \naround a quarter of an inch or below. Possible thunderstorms can\nproduce locally heavier rainfall. \n\nEnsembles for next week are showing some drying and slightly\nwarmer temps.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Confidence in rain chances this weekend are moderate. There is a\n  higher confidence in rainfall chances across the eastern zones\n  of the CWA. Less confidence moving further into western zones.\n\n- Confidence in rainfall totals remain somewhat low as models are\n  still uncertain on where the highest rainfall totals set up. \n\nKENZ",
